     <article class="story single" data-beat="sports">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPORTS</span> · BBC Sport · 2026-10-02 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Charlie Harmison signs professional deal with Durham</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#sports</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Durham has signed 18-year-old fast bowler Charlie Harmison to a two-year professional contract. The son of 2005 Ashes winner Steve Harmison, he has previously represented England at the Under-19 level.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/charlie-harmison-signs-professional-deal-with-durham.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Harmison is the fourth son of the 2005 Ashes-winning squad to sign professional terms with a County Championship club, joining the sons of Michael Vaughan and Andrew Flintoff. The signing coincides with Durham's return to the top flight after winning the Division Two title. Wicketkeeper-batter Robbie Bowman and batter Luke Symington also joined the club on rookie deals.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive coverage of the signing, including the correct context regarding the other sons of the 2005 Ashes squad.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/cricket/articles/c63r5dd3zgyro?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC Sport</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
