       <h1>Court orders refund for Delhi couple in holiday membership dispute</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A Delhi consumer commission has ruled against Royalty Holidays International after a local couple was misled into purchasing a Rs 65,000 holiday membership. The couple alleged that the company used unfair trade practices and provided false information regarding benefits and terms during a promotional event.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/court-orders-refund-for-delhi-couple-in-holiday-membership-d.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Finding the company guilty of deficient service, the commission ordered a full refund of the membership fee. Additionally, the court mandated that the firm pay compensation to the couple for the mental distress caused by the deceptive marketing practices.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Concise and accurate reporting of the consumer commission's ruling and the underlying dispute.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/legal/news/free-hotel-stays-promised-delhi-couple-signs-rs-65000-holiday-membership-court-orders-relief-over-fake-assurances/articleshow/134632825.cms" rel="noopener">Times of India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
