     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-02 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Crew-13 astronauts reach International Space Station in record eight-hour flight</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#space</span> <span>#NASA</span> <span>#SpaceX</span> <span>#International Space Station</span></div>
       <p>Four astronauts arrived at the International Space Station on Thursday, October 1, 2026, after completing an eight-hour express flight that set a U.S. speed record. The rapid transit was made possible by the space station's favorable positioning at the time of the late morning liftoff from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/crew-13-astronauts-reach-international-space-station-in-reco.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The mission, designated Crew-13, follows a three-week delay caused by a leaking fuel system in the Dragon capsule. Commander Jessica Watkins, the first Black woman to lead a crew to orbit, is joined by NASA’s Luke Delaney, Canada’s Joshua Kutryk, and Russia’s Sergey Teteryatnikov. The team will spend six months aboard the orbiting laboratory.</p><p>Joshua Kutryk is expected to become the fourth astronaut to become a father while in orbit, with his wife Heather due to give birth in late November. The crew carried a small crocheted dragon named 'Lucky' to commemorate their mission, which pays homage to the historic Apollo 13. This launch marked the 13th crew SpaceX has sent for NASA.</p><p>What to watch: The upcoming birth of Joshua Kutryk’s child while he remains on the six-month mission.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately summarizes the mission details, including the specific crew members and the context of the flight, while adhering to the source material.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-astronauts-international-space-station-quickest.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
