     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-02 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Dried Fish Narrative Emerges in Nagaon By-Election</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Nagaon Lok Sabha by-election, scheduled for October 6, has seen candidates trade barbs over food habits. AIUDF chief Badruddin Ajmal targeted Congress MP Rakibul Hussain by referencing his consumption of dried fish, a comment interpreted as a cultural slur.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/dried-fish-narrative-emerges-in-nagaon-by-election.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Chief Minister Himanta Sarma joined the rhetoric, comparing Hussain to a mythological demon with an insatiable appetite and criticizing his diet. In response, Hussain defended his choices and questioned the Chief Minister's own food habits. The seat, a former Congress stronghold, faces a contest following the resignation of the incumbent MP, who defected to the BJP.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft captures the core political tension and the specific food-related rhetoric mentioned in the source material accurately.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/assam/nagaon-lok-sabha-byelection-dried-fish-follows-biryani-in-campaign/article71535914.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
