     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · India Today · 2026-10-02 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Election Commission orders drive to re-enrol missing voters</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Election Commission has directed state officials to launch a one-month special drive to identify and re-enrol eligible voters omitted during the recent Special Intensive Revision. Booth Level Officers must now cross-check electoral rolls with pre-revision lists and conduct house-to-house verifications to address concerns over disenfranchisement.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/election-commission-orders-drive-to-re-enrol-missing-voters.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This initiative targets first-time voters and those mistakenly removed from the rolls, with a goal of processing 20 to 25 electors weekly. The Commission, which previously pruned 10.2 percent of the combined voters' list across nine states and three Union territories, will share the updated lists with political party agents to ensure transparency.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive coverage of the EC's directive, including the reasoning, the specific targets for BLOs, and the political context mentioned in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.indiatoday.in/india/story/election-commission-fresh-drive-re-enrol-left-out-new-voters-after-roll-revision-ptag-3007877-2026-10-02?utm_source=rss" rel="noopener">India Today</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
