     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-02 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Former Rajasthan Minister Vishvendra Singh Resigns from Congress</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Politics</span> <span>#Rajasthan</span> <span>#Congress</span> <span>#Vishvendra Singh</span></div>
       <p>Former Rajasthan Tourism Minister Vishvendra Singh announced his resignation from the Congress party on Friday, October 2, 2026. A member of the erstwhile Bharatpur royal family, Mr. Singh stated his decision was guided by his conscience and a refusal to accept &quot;wrong policies,&quot; adding he was hurt by statements from some party leaders. The announcement was made via a social media post.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/former-rajasthan-minister-vishvendra-singh-resigns-from-cong.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The move follows a sharp attack by Mr. Singh two days prior on State president Govind Singh Dotasra regarding the recent urban local body elections. Mr. Singh had blamed the Congress's Bharatpur and Deeg district presidents for the party's poor performance, alleging tickets were distributed to favorites while genuine workers were sidelined. In these polls, Congress secured 3,613 wards compared to the BJP's 4,179, with the BJP winning six of seven mayoral contests.</p><p>Mr. Singh subsequently demanded Mr. Dotasra's resignation and sought an investigation into allegations concerning recruitment examinations and benefits linked to the PCC chief's family. Responding on Thursday, October 1, Mr. Dotasra dismissed reports of a rift as &quot;BJP's toolkit&quot; propaganda. The Congress party has not yet formally confirmed Mr. Singh's resignation submission.</p><p>What to watch: The Congress's official response and potential political realignments in Rajasthan.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Thoroughly covers the resignation, the political context, and the specific claims made by both parties involved.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/rajasthan/can-no-longer-accept-wrong-policies-ex-rajasthan-minister-vishvendra-singh-quits-congress/article71536311.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-10-02/former-rajasthan-minister-vishvendra-singh-resigns-from-cong" data-title="Former Rajasthan Minister Vishvendra Singh Resigns from Congress"><b>Share:</b>
         <a class="sh-wa" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg class="ic" width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M8 1.8A6.2 6.2 0 0 0 2.7 11L1.8 14.2l3.3-.9A6.2 6.2 0 1 0 8 1.8z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>WhatsApp</a> ·
         <a class="sh-tg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg class="ic" width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M14.4 1.6 1.6 7l3.6 1.2L6 13l2.2-3.1 3.4 2.4 2.8-10.7z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Telegram</a> ·
