       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-02 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Irani Cup 2026: Rest of India Trail Jammu &amp; Kashmir by 17 Runs on Day 2</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#Irani Cup</span> <span>#Domestic Cricket</span></div>
       <p>The Irani Cup 2026 saw an impressive display of batting and bowling on Friday, October 2, at the Sher-i-Kashmir Stadium in Srinagar. At the close of Day 2, Rest of India trailed Jammu and Kashmir by 17 runs. Jammu and Kashmir captain Paras Dogra was a standout performer, scoring a crucial 75 runs off 143 balls, including six fours and one six, anchoring his team's resurgence.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/irani-cup-2026-rest-of-india-trail-jammu-kashmir-by-17-runs-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Dogra forged a vital 101-run partnership with Abdul Samad, who contributed 57 runs, laying a strong foundation for J&amp;K. Lone Nasir Muzaffar further bolstered the innings with an impressive 64, ensuring the team added significant runs to their total and strengthened their position in the contest.</p><p>For Rest of India, left-arm pacer Mukesh Choudhary delivered a superb bowling performance, making excellent use of the conditions. He claimed a five-wicket haul, finishing with figures of 5/78, dismissing key batsmen including Qamran Iqbal, Shubham Pundir, Shubham Khajuria, Paras Dogra, and Kanhaiya Wadhawan. Choudhary received strong support from Akash Deep, who took three wickets for 43 runs, and Mukesh Kumar, who added two wickets for 84 runs.</p><p>What to watch: The third day's play as Rest of India look to overcome the deficit.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article is a complete and accurate summary of the provided text, capturing all key statistical and narrative details.</p>
