     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-02 · editor 6/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Israel Honors Captain Smit Machchhar With Mumbai Billboards</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Consulate General of Israel in Mumbai has launched a public awareness campaign, installing billboards across the city to honor Captain Smit Machchhar. The displays, located at major junctions including Warden Road and the Western Express Highway, thank the pilot for saving 174 lives during the recent Flydubai cockpit incident. Machchhar, who sustained severe injuries, is being hailed as a hero in both India and Israel.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/israel-honors-captain-smit-machchhar-with-mumbai-billboards.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Consul General Yaniv Revach stated that Machchhar’s ability to activate emergency alerts and open the cockpit door prevented a potential diplomatic crisis. Israeli authorities are currently coordinating with the UAE government on the ongoing investigation into the attack, which remains under thorough review.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: While informative, it fails to mention the crucial detail that Israeli passengers also helped save the flight, focusing solely on the pilot.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/mumbai/israel-honours-captain-smit-machchhars-valour-with-billboards-in-mumbai/article71536325.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
