     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-10-02 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Jagadish Chandra Bose’s Crescograph Turned Invisible Plant Growth Visible</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Jagadish Chandra Bose</span> <span>#Crescograph</span> <span>#Science History</span></div>
       <p>More than a century ago, Indian physicist Jagadish Chandra Bose, born on 30 November 1858 in Mymensingh, devised the crescograph to capture plant movements too slow for the naked eye. Trained in physics and already renowned for work on electromagnetic waves, Bose taught at Presidency College, Calcutta, and later founded the Bose Research Institute. His instrument amplified tiny growth at the plant tip, first using two levers each providing a 100‑fold increase, achieving a combined 10,000‑fold magnification. In later versions, a magnetic system boosted sensitivity, allowing magnifications into the millions and recording movements as fine marks. Bose noted that plant growth could be as slow as one‑hundred‑thousandth of an inch per second, making the crescograph essential for measurable observations. He applied the device to species such as Mimosa pudica and Desmodium gyrans, studying electrical and mechanical responses under varied stimuli. By 1917 he submitted his high‑magnification work to the Royal Society, with findings published in the 1919 Proceedings of the Royal Society B. The crescograph established a quantitative approach to plant physiology that endures today.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/jagadish-chandra-boses-crescograph-turned-invisible-plant-gr.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Why it matters: precise measurement tools transform our understanding of slow natural processes.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is an excellent, comprehensive summary that incorporates all technical and historical details from the source text.</p>
