     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-02 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>JPC Defers Proposed Supreme Court Visit Amid Separation of Powers Concerns</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A Joint Parliamentary Committee (JPC) has deferred a planned visit to the Supreme Court, which was intended to discuss the 'One Nation, One Election' Bills. The proposal had sparked objections from Opposition MPs, who argued that consulting sitting judges on legislation that could later be subject to judicial review violates the separation of powers.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/jpc-defers-proposed-supreme-court-visit-amid-separation-of-p.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>While the JPC, led by BJP MP P.P. Chaudhary, claimed the cancellation was due to scheduling conflicts, critics like Rajya Sabha member Kapil Sibal labeled the interaction unconstitutional. The committee is currently reviewing the Constitution (129th Amendment) Bill and the Union Territories Laws (Amendment) Bill.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a clear, concise overview of the committee's deferred visit and the underlying separation of powers debate.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/parliamentary-panel-studying-simultaneous-elections-proposed-supreme-court-visit-why-is-separation-of-powers-an-issue/article71535975.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
