       <h1>NCERT Chapter to Include ‘Ram Rajya,’ NEP, and Sengol in Governance Curriculum</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#education</span> <span>#curriculum</span> <span>#NCERT</span> <span>#governance</span></div>
       <p>Students studying the National Council of Educational Research and Training (NCERT) curriculum will soon learn about 'Ram Rajya,' the National Education Policy (NEP), and 'Sengol' within a dedicated chapter on 'Governance and Public Policy.' This addition signifies a broadening of the topics covered under public administration and civics education in schools.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/ncert-chapter-to-include-ram-rajya-nep-and-sengol-in-governa.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The inclusion of 'Ram Rajya' in the curriculum suggests an exploration of traditional governance concepts. Simultaneously, the National Education Policy (NEP) will be studied, providing students with insights into contemporary educational reforms and their impact on the country's future human resource development and career pathways.</p><p>Furthermore, the 'Sengol,' a historical symbol, will be incorporated, likely to teach students about its significance in governance and public policy contexts. This updated content within the NCERT framework aims to provide a comprehensive understanding of both historical and modern aspects of governance crucial for future citizens and professionals.</p><p>What to watch: The specific details and pedagogical approach used to teach these concepts within the NCERT chapter.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the source summary provided.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
