     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-10-02 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>New Mexico Seeks Up to $40 Billion Penalty from Meta</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>New Mexico authorities are asking a judge to impose $35 billion to $40 billion in penalties against Meta following a jury verdict regarding the Cambridge Analytica scandal. Jurors previously found Meta misled residents regarding data privacy and misinformation, identifying 43 million violations of state consumer protection laws.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/new-mexico-seeks-up-to-40-billion-penalty-from-meta.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>State attorney Randi McGinn argued that financial penalties must be significant enough to impact Meta’s stock price and behavior. Meta, which requested the court cap penalties at $3.45 billion, contends the state failed to prove residents were misled. A final judicial decision is expected later this month.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately summarizes the penalty dispute and the context of the jury verdict.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
