     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Economic Times · 2026-10-02 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Pakistan Government and PTI Hold Talks Amidst March Plans</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Pakistan</span> <span>#Politics</span> <span>#PTI</span> <span>#Government</span></div>
       <p>The federal government and leaders of Pakistan Tehreek-e-Insaf (PTI) engaged in talks on Friday, aiming to de-escalate political tensions ahead of the opposition party's planned long march to Islamabad. These negotiations, facilitated by National Assembly Speaker Sardar Ayaz Sadiq, occurred a week after PTI announced its march would commence on October 4, following a one-week postponement.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/pakistan-government-and-pti-hold-talks-amidst-march-plans.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>A six-member government delegation, led by Prime Minister's Adviser on Political Affairs Rana Sanaullah, participated, including Interior Minister Mohsin Naqvi. The PTI delegation featured Chairman Barrister Gohar Ali Khan and Leader of the Opposition in the Senate, Allama Raja Nasir Abbas. PTI international spokesman Zulfiqar Bukhari confirmed the talks, stating their primary goal was to address the political situation surrounding the planned October 4 protest.</p><p>PTI presented key demands, including an immediate meeting with jailed former Prime Minister Imran Khan and his transfer to a hospital for examination by personal doctors. They also sought the release of Khan's sisters and other arrested individuals, along with a proper medical check-up for Bushra Bibi. Rana Sanaullah emphasized that solutions lie in negotiation, not protests, and that courts handle Khan's cases.</p><p>The long march is slated to begin from Gandi Chowk in Lakki Marwat, with stops in Karak and Kohat on October 5, before entering Peshawar district. Despite the negotiations, Bukhari stated the &quot;call for long march is intact,&quot; even as the government has increased security, placing shipping containers on Islamabad routes and mobilizing additional police.</p><p>What to watch: The outcome of further negotiations and the status of the planned long march.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately and comprehensively synthesizes the provided source material regarding the political talks and protest plans.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/international/world-news/pak-government-holds-talks-with-imran-khans-party-to-defuse-tensions-ahead-of-long-march-to-islamabad/articleshow/134639496.cms" rel="noopener">Economic Times</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
