     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · News18 India · 2026-10-02 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Stalin Warns TVK Government Over Hindi Signboard Row in Tamil Nadu</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Tamil Nadu</span> <span>#Language Policy</span> <span>#MK Stalin</span> <span>#TVK</span></div>
       <p>DMK president MK Stalin has issued a strong warning to the Tamilaga Vettri Kazhagam (TVK) government, cautioning against any actions that could undermine Tamil Nadu’s established two-language policy. This intervention follows the discovery of Hindi text alongside Tamil and English on a newly installed highway signboard in Thoothukudi district. In response, the Tamil Nadu government promptly removed the Hindi text and suspended two Highways Department officials responsible.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/stalin-warns-tvk-government-over-hindi-signboard-row-in-tami.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Stalin questioned the state government's role, stating on X (formerly Twitter), &quot;Until now, Tamil Nadu has been facing the Union government’s Hindi imposition. Now, it seems the state government itself is doing that job.&quot; He further speculated if this was a &quot;test the waters&quot; attempt by the TVK government. The DMK leader also criticized the TVK's &quot;reels administration&quot; and delivered a direct message: &quot;Two-language policy is Tamil Nadu’s life policy. Don’t play with it.&quot;</p><p>The controversial signboard was located on the Sathankulam-Nazareth State Highway (SH-93). The suspended officials, an Assistant Divisional Engineer and an Assistant Engineer, face departmental proceedings for acting contrary to the state’s two-language policy. Tamil Nadu has upheld a two-language policy of Tamil and English since 1968, consistently resisting Hindi imposition, making this a politically sensitive issue.</p><p>What to watch: The ongoing political discourse and adherence to Tamil Nadu's language policy by the state government.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately summarizes the political controversy, the specific actions taken by the government, and the context of the two-language policy.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/india/dont-play-with-tamil-nadus-two-language-policy-stalin-warns-vijays-tvk-govt-over-signboard-row-ws-l-10364199.html" rel="noopener">News18 India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-10-02/stalin-warns-tvk-government-over-hindi-signboard-row-in-tami" data-title="Stalin Warns TVK Government Over Hindi Signboard Row in Tamil Nadu"><b>Share:</b>
