     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-02 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Study Finds AI-Assisted Corporate Disclosures Sound Increasing Similar</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A study published in Academy of Management Proceedings reveals that corporate earnings presentations are becoming stylistically uniform due to widespread AI tool usage. Researchers found that AI-influenced text appears more boilerplate, which investors often perceive as less informative and firm-specific, leading to negative market reactions.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/study-finds-ai-assisted-corporate-disclosures-sound-increasi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Conversely, live Q&amp;A sessions remain distinct because their spontaneous nature is less susceptible to AI preparation. Authors suggest that while AI can improve communication efficiency, it risks smoothing away the individual voice and specific details that provide credibility to corporate disclosures.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Concise and accurate representation of the study findings regarding AI-assisted corporate disclosures.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-ai-corporate-communication.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
