     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-10-02 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Tesla Introduces Emergency Drive Away Feature for Superchargers</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Tesla</span> <span>#Supercharger</span> <span>#Emergency Drive Away</span></div>
       <p>Tesla has rolled out a new &quot;Emergency Drive Away&quot; feature, allowing drivers to quickly detach from a Supercharger and drive away during urgent situations. This development follows an incident where a driver was shot inside a Tesla taxi while charging at an In-N-Out Burger location in Twin Falls, Idaho. Previously, disconnecting required unlocking the charge port via the vehicle's infotainment system, an app, or a button on the charger handle, which could be problematic in emergencies.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/tesla-introduces-emergency-drive-away-feature-for-supercharg.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The new system activates if a driver shifts into Drive while still plugged in, prompting a notice on the infotainment screen. Users must hold the brake pedal and tap &quot;Activate&quot; to override the lock. Tesla has issued warnings that using the feature will cause damage to both the vehicle and the Supercharger station, emphasizing its use &quot;only in an emergency.&quot;</p><p>Currently, the Emergency Drive Away feature is exclusively available for Superchargers within the United States. It requires the latest software update, and older models, specifically pre-2021 Model S and X vehicles, are not supported. Tesla stated on X that deliberate misuse &quot;will invoke additional penalties,&quot; with potential &quot;electrical and civil cost&quot; reaching up to $25,000.</p><p>What to watch: How widely this feature is adopted and its impact on charging safety.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft comprehensively covers all facts provided in the source text, including the specific technical requirements, limitations, and the context of the incident.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2275509/tesla-supercharger-emergency-drive-away-feature/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
