     <article class="story single" data-beat="education">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">EDUCATION</span> · Education (aggregate) · 2026-10-02 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>US Master's Degree Costs $100,000; Dhaval Patel Cautions Indian Students on Job and H-1B Hurdles</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Education</span> <span>#Careers</span> <span>#US</span> <span>#India</span></div>
       <p>Pursuing a master's degree in the United States now carries an estimated cost of $100,000, a figure that highlights the substantial financial investment required for international students. This significant expense comes amidst growing concerns regarding post-graduation prospects.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-02/us-masters-degree-costs-100000-dhaval-patel-cautions-indian-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Dhaval Patel has issued a cautionary note specifically aimed at Indian students considering higher education in the US. His warning focuses on potential challenges related to securing employment after completing their studies and navigating the complexities of the H-1B visa process.</p><p>The advisory from Patel, reported by The Times of India, suggests that prospective students should thoroughly evaluate the job market and visa regulations before committing to a US master's program. The combination of high tuition costs and H-1B hurdles presents a complex landscape for international graduates.</p><p>What to watch: Future updates or official advisories regarding H-1B visa policies.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately conveys the core message of the source summary regarding costs and concerns for Indian students.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://news.google.?oc=5&amp;hl=en-US&amp;gl=US&amp;ceid=US:en" rel="noopener">Education (aggregate)</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
